    \sigma_n := \max_{1 \leq k \leq d_n} |\tau_k -\psi|
\end{equation}
as the maximal effect heterogeneity. Note that $\sigma_n=0$ corresponds to the constant conditional average treatment effects and $\sigma_n=2$ imposes no restriction on the model class. Our parameterization can interpolate between these two extremes as $\sigma_n$ varies. 

\subsection{Upper Bound}

The estimator we propose to take advantage of the effect homogeneity is 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:homo-est}
    \hat{\tau} =  \frac{\sum_{k=1}^d \hat{t}_k \hat{\tau}_k}{\sum_{k=1}^d \hat{t}_k}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{t}_k = \hat{p}_kI( 0 < \hat{\pi}_k <1) $ is the indicator of whether both treated and untreated samples are observed in the $k$-th level and $\hat{\tau}_k = \hat{\mu}_{1k}-\hat{\mu}_{0k}$. The idea is to only estimate the CATE $\tau_k$ within those categories with both treated and untreated units and take a weighted average over such categories.  We restrict our attention to these categories so that unbiased estimation of $(\mu_{1k}, \mu_{0k})$ (and hence $\tau_k$) is possible. We again define $0/0=0$ if $\sum_{k=1}^d \hat{t}_k = 0$, i.e. there is no such category that contains both treated and untreated units. Clearly on the event $\left\{\sum_{k=1}^d \hat{t}_k = 0\right\}$, we cannot obtain any information on treatment effects from $\hat{\tau}$. Under appropriate scaling conditions, the probability of this ``adverse" event converges to $0$ even in the case $n \lesssim d$, as summarized in the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:nocollision}
The chance of having every category consist of either all treated or all untreated units (i.e., no collisions of any treated and untreated units at any category) is upper-bounded as 
\[
\MP\left(\sum_{k=1}^d \hat{t}_k = 0\right) \leq 2 \exp\left(-C (\epsilon) \frac{n^2}{n \vee d} \right)
\]
where $C(\epsilon)> 0$ is a constant depending on $\epsilon$.
\end{lemma}
This lemma is not only critical to our analysis of $\hat{\tau}$ but also of independent interests in the literature of applied probability. It is closely related to the birthday problem \citep{clevenson1991majorization} and can be viewed as the occupancy problem \citep{wendl2003collision, nakata2014number} under a different sampling scheme. In the classic occupancy problem, the number of treated and untreated units are fixed first and then they are assigned to different categories of $X$ with probability vectors $\bp^{\text{T}}$ and $\bp^{\text{C}}$ separately. While in our setting people's covariates are first sampled, following which their treatment assignments are determined. Importantly, Lemma \ref{lemma:nocollision} shows the probability that the denominator of $\hat{\tau}$ is $0$ vanishes even in the high-dimensional regime $n \lesssim d$, as long as $d/n^2 \rightarrow 0$. With Lemma \ref{lemma:nocollision} we can derive the following theorem bounding the MSE of $\hat{\tau}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:homogeneity}
    The bias and variance of $\hat{\tau}$ are bounded as 
    \begin{equation*}
        |\ME[\hat{\tau}-\psi]| \leq \sigma_n + 2 \exp\left( 
-C(\epsilon)\frac{n^2}{n \vee d} \right),
    \end{equation*}
    \[
    \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\tau}) \lesssim  \sigma_n^2  + \frac{ d}{n^2} +\frac{1}{n},
    \]
    where $C(\epsilon)$ and the constant behind ``$\lesssim$" depend on $\epsilon$.
    % If we further assume that there exists a constant $\alpha \in (0,1]$ such that $p_k \geq \alpha/d$ for all $k \in [d]$, then in the regime $n\leq \frac{3\epsilon(1-\epsilon)d}{\alpha}$, we have
    % \[
    % \ME \left[ \operatorname{Var}\left(\hat{\tau} \big| \bX^n, \bA^n\right) \right] \leq \frac{4d}{\alpha^2 \epsilon(1-\epsilon)n^2} + \frac{3}{2}\exp \left( - \frac{\alpha^2 \epsilon(1-\epsilon)n^2}{2d}\right) + \exp\left(-C (\epsilon) \frac{n^2}{n \vee d} \right).
    % \]
    As a consequence, the estimator $\hat{\tau}$ is consistent if $\sigma_n \rightarrow 0$ and $d/n^2 \rightarrow 0$ in the regime $n \lesssim d$. 
\end{theorem}

statements.proof_tex:
Fix \(0<\epsilon<1/2\), \(0\le\sigma\le2\), and integers \(n,d\ge1\).  Shrink the constants supplied by Theorems~\(\mathrm{thm:frontier\text{-}upper}\) and \(\mathrm{thm:radius\text{-}channel\text{-}converse}\), if necessary, so that the common dimension condition
\[
 1\le d\le c_{\epsilon}\frac{n^2}{\log(en)}
\tag{1}
\]
implies the range conditions of both theorems.  Enlarge the common upper constant and decrease the common lower constant once more, if necessary, so that \(0<c_{\epsilon}\le C_{\epsilon}<\infty\).

By Definition~\(\mathrm{def:total\text{-}estimator}\), the selected map \(\widehat\tau_n^{\star}\) is an element of \(\mathcal T_{n,M}\).  It is therefore an admissible witness in the infimum defining \(\mathsf R_{n,d,\epsilon,M,\sigma}\).  Definition~\(\mathrm{def:minimax\text{-}risk}\) and Theorem~\(\mathrm{thm:frontier\text{-}upper}\) give, in this order,
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf R_{n,d,\epsilon,M,\sigma}
 &=\inf_{T\in\mathcal T_{n,M}}
   \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{d,\epsilon,M,\sigma}}
   E_{Q_P^{(n)}}[(T-\tau(P))^2]\\
 &\le
   \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{d,\epsilon,M,\sigma}}
   E_{Q_P^{(n)}}[(\widehat\tau_n^{\star}-\tau(P))^2]\\
 &\le C_{\epsilon}M^2r_{n,d,\sigma}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]
This is a genuine data-driven minimax upper bound: the estimator depends only on the observed sample and the fixed known inputs \(n,d,M,\sigma\), and its clipping and all division-boundary fallbacks are part of Definition~\(\mathrm{def:total\text{-}estimator}\) and its two construction inputs.

On the other hand, Theorem~\(\mathrm{thm:radius\text{-}channel\text{-}converse}\), whose uniform finite-sample range includes (1), yields
\[
 \mathsf R_{n,d,\epsilon,M,\sigma}
 \ge c_{\epsilon}M^2\left\{
 \frac1n+\frac d{n^2}
 +\sigma^2\min\left(1,\frac{d^2}{n^2\log^2(en)}\right)
 \right\}.
\tag{3}
\]
The parametric component used in that converse is also valid uniformly by Lemma~\(\mathrm{lem:parametric\text{-}lower}\), including every boundary value of \(\sigma\) and every positive integer \(n\).

Both (2) and (3) quantify over the identical class \(\mathcal P_{d,\epsilon,M,\sigma}\) appearing in Definition~\(\mathrm{def:minimax\text{-}risk}\).  By Definition~\(\mathrm{def:model\text{-}class}\), this is precisely the arbitrary-cell-mass real-outcome class with fixed overlap, the conditional-mean normalization, and the conditional-second-central-moment envelope; zero-mass cells require no conditional quantities.  Thus no class substitution, bounded-outcome restriction, positivity strengthening, or additional rank condition enters either side.  Combining (2) and (3) proves the displayed two-sided bracket.

Finally, the two inequalities themselves do not assert that their normalized right-hand expressions are uniformly order-equivalent.  The lower radius term is the product \(\sigma^2\min(1,u)\), whereas the selected upper radius term is \(\min(1,u,\sigma^2+d/n^2)\), with \(u=d^2/[n^2\log^2(en)]\).  Hence the theorem proves exactly the claimed bracket and preserves, rather than resolves, the stated possible shrinking-radius mismatch.  The final paragraph of the statement records theorem-level provenance and positioning; it does not enlarge the mathematical claim proved in (2)--(3).

